WEBVTT

1
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.600
The universe is everything that surrounds us.

2
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.500
It is everything that was before us, and everything that will be after us.

3
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:49.300
A quiet voice deep in our subconscious has always awakened in us a primal curiosity about the nature of the universe and its greatest mysteries.

4
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.500
The size and age of the universe are beyond human understanding,

5
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:59.500
and it is right there, in this infinite space and time, that planet Earth, our little home, hides.

6
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:40.700
We humans, although we have existed only recently compared to the origin of the universe,

7
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:46.000
are very curious, brave and thirst for new discoveries that will give us answers

8
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.700
to some of the most important questions about the universe and our place in its boundless expanse.

9
00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:01.700
The very future of our species depends precisely on how well we understand the universe in which we float, like a speck of dust on the celestial horizon.

10
00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:19.310
This small journey through the mysteries of space on the Gajbotron channel should not replace your formal education on this subject, but only inspire you to want to find out and learn more yourself, regardless of your age or personal beliefs.

11
00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:33.490
This is a story about everything that surrounds us, and at the same time a story about ourselves. On this journey we will need imagination, skepticism, but above all a lot of science and scientific facts.

12
00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:55.200
The progress of modern science in exploring the universe and its laws has been achieved above all thanks to generations of researchers and scientists, as well as a few simple rules.

13
00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.560
Check and test ideas and theories through experiments and observations.

14
00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:09.540
Base further research on theories that prove correct or successful in testing, and discard theories that fail the test.

15
00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:16.380
Follow the scientific evidence wherever it leads, and constantly double-check all new facts.

16
00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:27.600
Have you ever wondered where we are in our universe?

17
00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:35.960
From the point of view of time, on the timeline behind us is the past and ahead is the future.

18
00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:43.100
If, however, we consider the universe in the context of space, and if we want to explore its farthest corners,

19
00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:48.020
then first of all we need to know what our cosmic residential address is.

20
00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:54.100
Our first neighbor is the Moon.

21
00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:57.700
Without atmosphere, without water and without life.

22
00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:05.280
It quietly orbits the Earth and with its many scars reminds us of ancient times of destructive cosmic collisions.

23
00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.760
However, the central place in our neighborhood is occupied by the Sun.

24
00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:21.060
A star 4.5 billion years old.

25
00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:33.460
It drives almost all life on Earth, affects our winds and waves, and with its powerful gravity keeps all the planets of the Solar System near it.

26
00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:41.750
The closest to the Sun is Mercury. It is the smallest planet in our system.

27
00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:54.710
If we keep moving away from the Sun, we run into the next planet, cloud-covered Venus, where due to the greenhouse effect a truly hellish atmosphere eventually arose.

28
00:04:55.210 --> 00:05:05.120
Our home, planet Earth, is right after Venus.

29
00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:11.840
However, unlike it, we were lucky to be in a zone suitable for life,

30
00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:16.000
in the so-called Goldilocks zone.

31
00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:21.860
Not too hot and not too cold, but just right.

32
00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:26.080
On we go.

33
00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:40.500
Next is Mars, a red planet with a solid surface, like Earth. Although it is dry as a desert, a long time ago Mars also had rivers, seas and an atmosphere, but over time it lost them.

34
00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:50.900
When we pass Mars's orbit, we run into a belt where asteroids float and revolve. Here there is sometimes quite a big traffic jam.

35
00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:56.180
Moving even farther from the Sun, we run into the next neighbor.

36
00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:04.040
With its four giant moons and a bunch of smaller ones, Jupiter resembles a kind of solar system in miniature.

37
00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:11.200
Besides, Jupiter's mass is greater than the mass of all the other planets of the Solar System combined.

38
00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:16.320
What everyone recognizes Jupiter by is, of course, its giant red spot.

39
00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:23.320
It's actually a hurricane the size of three Earths, which has been raging on Jupiter for hundreds of years.

40
00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:34.900
We leave Jupiter behind and arrive at the real jewel of our Solar System — Saturn.

41
00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:41.460
It is surrounded by countless chunks of ice and rock that revolve around this giant,

42
00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:45.720
thus forming Saturn's recognizable disk, that is, ring.

43
00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:50.620
Few planets can boast such an adornment.

44
00:06:50.680 --> 00:07:04.580
If we go further, after Saturn we run into Uranus and Neptune, the most distant planets of our system.

45
00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:11.440
These planets were completely unknown to our ancient ancestors until the invention of the first telescopes.

46
00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:22.830
If you go even farther beyond these planets, you'll run into many ice giants floating in the boundless expanses of space,

47
00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:31.610
and among them Pluto, which recently lost its planet status because of its small size and now belongs to the dwarf planets.

48
00:07:32.470 --> 00:07:36.990
Continuing our journey, we gradually enter interstellar space.

49
00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:45.230
Of all the craft humans have launched into space, the famous probe "Voyager 1" reached and passed here in 2012,

50
00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:53.650
which continued its way even farther, to the unexplored expanses of the universe and the countless new worlds hiding somewhere out there.

51
00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:56.010
And along this path lies something else.

52
00:07:57.010 --> 00:08:02.770
From this distance our Sun looks like just another distant star in a sea of similar ones,

53
00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:09.990
but it still strongly influences this surroundings gravitationally, and therefore the trillions of frozen comets,

54
00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:16.930
which are actually small, five-billion-year-old remnants from the time when the Solar System was forming.

55
00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:26.670
This icy debris now together forms a thin shell around our whole Solar System and is called the Oort cloud.

56
00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:35.550
This cloud is the boundary of the Solar System, and therefore the second station on our journey through space.

57
00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:53.300
In recent decades, with the development of technology on Earth, we have gained the ability, using powerful telescopes, to see even better and even farther into the boundless expanses of the universe.

58
00:08:53.800 --> 00:09:06.820
Countless stars similar to our Sun are the most noticeable, but these stars are usually surrounded by their own planets like our Earth, which orbit their stars and which are significantly more numerous than the stars themselves.

59
00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:14.420
So how many stars and planets are there really in our interstellar neighborhood and what does this neighborhood look like at all?

60
00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:22.140
Our Solar System, together with countless similar systems, is part of a larger whole called a galaxy.

61
00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:27.220
The galaxy in which our Solar System is located is called the Milky Way.

62
00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:36.620
The galaxy is so large that our system is barely noticeable in this cosmic spiral, which slowly rotates and travels through the universe.

63
00:09:36.820 --> 00:09:42.560
We are about 30,000 light-years from the center of our galaxy.

64
00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:48.340
What distance is that? More about that in the next episodes of this series.

65
00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:53.900
Our galaxy is also the third station on our path through space.

66
00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:58.640
If we move even farther away, we will see the first neighboring galaxies,

67
00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:02.660
and the first of them is the large spiral galaxy Andromeda.

68
00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:09.100
Together with our galaxy and several smaller ones it forms the Local Group of galaxies.

69
00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:13.100
The Local Group is the fourth station on this journey.

70
00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:17.420
We are moving away faster and faster from our home and our Sun,

71
00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:22.140
which in this infinity can no longer be seen, like our whole galaxy.

72
00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:27.080
For now before us are many other local groups of galaxies,

73
00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:30.480
and all of them together form a supercluster of galaxies.

74
00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:35.700
This supercluster in which we are located is called the Virgo Supercluster.

75
00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:44.480
From this point of view each tiniest dot we see is not a planet or a star, but a whole galaxy.

76
00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:54.580
Each galaxy contains billions of stars similar to our Sun, and countless planets orbiting these stars.

77
00:10:54.580 --> 00:11:01.860
However, this whole supercluster is actually only a tiny part of our universe.

78
00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:08.200
Lost in the infinity of space, our supercluster becomes almost invisible.

79
00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:19.400
The web formed by hundreds of billions of galaxies is the visible part of the universe that we know today, and at the same time our sixth station on this path.

80
00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:32.110
What is the visible universe? What is, in fact, the meaning of this concept?

81
00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:39.570
The thing is that there is a limit to how far we can see through space and time, or more precisely, through spacetime.

82
00:11:39.890 --> 00:11:42.570
This limit is called the cosmic horizon.

83
00:11:43.090 --> 00:11:49.850
Beyond this horizon lie parts of the universe that are simply too far away to be visible.

84
00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:50.870
Why?

85
00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:59.590
Because in 13.8 billion years — roughly the age of our universe — light from these distant regions

86
00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:08.970
has still not had time to travel all the way to us, that is, to our telescopes, which catch this light and create images from it.

87
00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:22.340
The visible universe is also our last station, but the journey itself is not over yet.

88
00:12:22.660 --> 00:12:30.920
It has only begun and will continue in the next episode of this series, when we will look at the whole universe through the prism of time.

89
00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:45.000
From its very origin, that is, the Big Bang, and up to the appearance of us, curious humans, living on a tiny blue planet hidden in the boundlessness of space, next to a star barely visible in our galaxy.

90
00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:52.000
Thank you all for watching; the Gajbotron channel and this whole project can be supported with a click on like and subscribe,

91
00:12:52.000 --> 00:13:05.880
next to the subscribe button turn on the notification bell, and in the description there are links for donations through Patreon and PayPal, as well as a link to membership for everyone who wants to support the work of the channel that way.

92
00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:07.780
Until the next episode!